target: 10.4.12.0/24   (the Crazy Ants subnet)

Find a host that's exposed to the outside but clearly meant to stay
internal — a staging box, a forgotten test server. That's the back
office.

    nmap 10.4.12.0/24        sweep the subnet
    nmap -sV 10.4.12.0/24    + service versions
    nmap 10.4.12.88          one host
    nmap --help              real nmap help

Long output? Pipe through less:    nmap -sV 10.4.12.0/24 | less


What recon is actually for
──────────────────────────
Recon isn't about "vulnerabilities." It's about human carelessness.
Somebody at this company did something they didn't think anybody
would notice — and left a trace.

Most of what nmap returns will be normal: their public website, the
mail server, the customer-facing API. These are well-locked-down and
supposed to be there. Skip past them.

What you want is the host that exists because somebody made a
decision they didn't clean up after. A test server spun up for a
sprint three years ago and never torn down. A staging environment
that was supposed to be internal but got accidentally exposed when
someone set up a port forward "just for today" and forgot.

When you have it:

    apply m0use{<hostname>}        or       apply m0use{<ip-address>}

Stuck?  cat hint  — no penalty.
